<section class="ro-hero" id="dial"><div class="ro-w ro-hero-g"><div><p class="ro-k">Oven roasting, made simple</p><h1>Heat, time and a hot pan.</h1><p class="ro-lead">Roasted is an independent cooking guide about the oven&rsquo;s best trick: high, dry heat that turns vegetables sweet, skins crisp and edges golden. Pick an ingredient on the dial to get its temperature and time.</p><div class="ro-pick" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Ingredient"><label><input type="radio" name="ing" value="potato" checked><span>Potatoes</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="ing" value="root"><span>Carrots &amp; roots</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="ing" value="brassica"><span>Broccoli &amp; sprouts</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="ing" value="squash"><span>Squash</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="ing" value="pepper"><span>Peppers &amp; onions</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="ing" value="chicken"><span>Whole chicken</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="ing" value="thighs"><span>Chicken thighs</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="ing" value="fish"><span>Fish fillets</span></label></div></div>
<div class="ro-dial"><svg viewBox="0 0 300 300" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="150" cy="150" r="140" fill="#2B2320"/><circle cx="150" cy="150" r="140" fill="none" stroke="#3C312C" stroke-width="16"/><path id="ro-arc" d="" fill="none" stroke="#E8601C" stroke-width="16" stroke-linecap="round"/><circle cx="150" cy="150" r="104" fill="#201A17"/></svg><div class="ro-dial-t" aria-live="polite"><b id="ro-tc">220&deg;C</b><span id="ro-tf">425&deg;F</span><em id="ro-tm">40&ndash;50 min</em></div><p class="ro-dial-n" id="ro-tip"></p></div></div></section>

<section class="ro-s"><div class="ro-w ro-anat"><div class="ro-hot"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1792736945475-840746066afe?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=520&q=75" alt="assorted vegetables including corn, zucchini and mushrooms" width="640" height="520" loading="lazy"><span class="ro-dot" style="left:18%;top:28%">1</span><span class="ro-dot" style="left:62%;top:22%">2</span><span class="ro-dot" style="left:44%;top:62%">3</span><span class="ro-dot" style="left:80%;top:70%">4</span></div><div><p class="ro-k">Anatomy of a good tray</p><h2>Why some trays roast and others steam</h2><ol class="ro-key"><li><b>Even pieces.</b> Cut everything to roughly the same size so it finishes together.</li><li><b>Space to breathe.</b> Leave gaps; crowded vegetables steam instead of browning.</li><li><b>A thin, even coat of oil.</b> Enough to shine, not to pool.</li><li><b>Hot metal.</b> A dark, heavy tray heated in the oven gives the best crust.</li></ol></div></div></section>

<section class="ro-s ro-paper"><div class="ro-w"><p class="ro-k">The method</p><h2>Six steps, every time</h2><ol class="ro-flow"><li><b>Prep</b><span>Cut evenly, dry well</span></li><li><b>Preheat</b><span>Oven and tray, 15 min</span></li><li><b>Season</b><span>Oil, salt, then spices</span></li><li><b>Roast</b><span>Middle shelf, hot oven</span></li><li><b>Turn</b><span>Once, halfway</span></li><li><b>Rest</b><span>Meat 10&ndash;20 min</span></li></ol></div></section>

<section class="ro-s"><div class="ro-w"><p class="ro-k">Know your heat</p><h2>Roast, bake, grill or broil?</h2><div class="tblw"><table class="ro-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Heat</th><th>Typical temperature</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Roast</td><td>Dry oven heat, all around</td><td>200&ndash;230&deg;C (400&ndash;450&deg;F)</td><td>Vegetables, whole birds, joints</td></tr><tr><td>Bake</td><td>Dry oven heat, moderate</td><td>160&ndash;190&deg;C (325&ndash;375&deg;F)</td><td>Breads, cakes, casseroles</td></tr><tr><td>Grill (outdoor)</td><td>Direct flame or coals below</td><td>Very hot, variable</td><td>Steaks, skewers, corn</td></tr><tr><td>Broil / grill (oven)</td><td>Intense heat from above</td><td>Top element, very hot</td><td>Melting, charring, finishing</td></tr><tr><td>Air fry</td><td>Small oven with strong fan</td><td>180&ndash;200&deg;C (350&ndash;400&deg;F)</td><td>Small batches, quick crisping</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="ro-s ro-dark"><div class="ro-w ro-ba"><div><p class="ro-k">What heat does</p><h2>Raw to roasted</h2><p class="ro-lead">Drag the slider. Roasting drives off water, browns natural sugars and turns firm, bitter vegetables soft and sweet.</p></div><div class="ro-bab" id="ba"><div class="ro-ba-i"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1792354079048-dd65bc74a9b8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=420&q=75" alt="slices of roasted eggplant with cheese" width="640" height="420" loading="lazy"></div><div class="ro-ba-top" id="ba-top"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1792736944363-597884d7fe4a?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=420&q=75" alt="slicing an eggplant on a board" width="640" height="420" loading="lazy"></div><input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" id="ba-r" aria-label="Compare raw and roasted"><span class="ro-ba-l">Raw</span><span class="ro-ba-r">Roasted</span></div></div></section>

<section class="ro-s"><div class="ro-w"><p class="ro-k">From the oven</p><h2>A tray of ideas</h2><div class="ro-mas"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1791217640572-4ee08526f32d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=520&q=75" alt="corn cob roasting over an open flame" width="420" height="520" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Corn, charred over an open flame</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794240280376-b5837fdd5cfe?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=320&q=75" alt="roasted pork knuckle with crispy skin" width="420" height="320" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Crisp-skinned pork, slow then hot</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793779474789-6d7e6c99e4e0?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=420&q=75" alt="slices of roasted duck with crispy skin" width="420" height="420" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Roast duck, sliced thin</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1792587545308-cf962b4b5931?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=520&q=75" alt="person holding a large platter of roasted food" width="420" height="520" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A full tray, straight from the oven</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793130500824-d68551ad32ab?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=320&q=75" alt="clay tagine filled with vegetables" width="420" height="320" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Vegetables, slow-cooked in clay</figcaption></figure></div></div></section>

<section class="ro-s ro-paper" id="pan"><div class="ro-w ro-pan"><div><p class="ro-k">Pan checker</p><h2>Will it fit on one tray?</h2><p class="ro-lead">Crowding is the most common roasting mistake. Tell us your tray and how much you are roasting.</p><label for="pc-s">Tray size<select id="pc-s"><option value="20">Quarter sheet (23 &times; 33 cm)</option><option value="40" selected>Half sheet (33 &times; 46 cm)</option><option value="80">Full sheet (46 &times; 66 cm)</option></select></label><label for="pc-c">Chopped vegetables (cups)<input id="pc-c" type="number" min="1" max="40" value="8"></label></div><div><div class="ro-grid" id="pc-g" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="ro-res" id="pc-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div></section>

<section class="ro-s"><div class="ro-w"><p class="ro-k">Through the year</p><h2>The roasting calendar</h2><div class="ro-cal"><div><h3>Spring</h3><p>Asparagus, new potatoes, spring onions, radishes</p><span>Short, very hot roasts</span></div><div><h3>Summer</h3><p>Peppers, courgettes, aubergine, tomatoes, corn</p><span>High heat, quick colour</span></div><div><h3>Autumn</h3><p>Squash, beetroot, mushrooms, carrots</p><span>Longer roasts, deeper caramel</span></div><div><h3>Winter</h3><p>Parsnips, sprouts, cauliflower, whole roasts</p><span>The season of the Sunday tray</span></div></div></div></section>

<section class="ro-s ro-dark"><div class="ro-w"><p class="ro-k">Recipe file</p><h2>Two guides worth keeping</h2><div class="ro-cards"><a href="roasting-temperatures-guide.html" class="ro-card"><span class="ro-tab">No. 01</span><b>Roasting Temperatures</b><em>Times and temperatures for vegetables, poultry, meat and fish, plus resting and safe internal temperatures.</em></a><a href="roast-vegetables-guide.html" class="ro-card"><span class="ro-tab">No. 02</span><b>Roast Vegetables</b><em>Cutting, oiling, seasoning and timing mixed trays so every vegetable finishes at its best.</em></a></div></div></section>

